1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,716
Picture a warm summer night in nineteen

2
00:00:02,716 --> 00:00:03,552
fifty-eight.

3
00:00:03,732 --> 00:00:06,273
The sky is turning purple over a

4
00:00:06,273 --> 00:00:08,655
cornfield at the edge of town.

5
00:00:08,835 --> 00:00:11,246
A line of cars stretches down the

6
00:00:11,246 --> 00:00:12,926
highway, headlights on,

7
00:00:12,926 --> 00:00:14,315
waiting to pull in.

8
00:00:14,495 --> 00:00:17,204
Kids in pajamas are bouncing in the

9
00:00:17,204 --> 00:00:17,978
back seat.

10
00:00:18,158 --> 00:00:19,124
And up ahead,

11
00:00:19,124 --> 00:00:20,983
glowing against the dark,

12
00:00:20,983 --> 00:00:22,767
is a giant white screen.

13
00:00:22,947 --> 00:00:25,593
Tonight, the whole family is going to

14
00:00:25,593 --> 00:00:26,523
the drive-in.

15
00:00:26,873 --> 00:00:27,789
At their peak,

16
00:00:27,789 --> 00:00:29,689
in the late nineteen fifties,

17
00:00:29,689 --> 00:00:32,505
there were more than four thousand drive-in

18
00:00:32,505 --> 00:00:33,815
theaters in America.

19
00:00:33,995 --> 00:00:36,822
Almost every town of any size had

20
00:00:36,822 --> 00:00:37,165
one.

21
00:00:37,345 --> 00:00:39,216
For a generation of Americans,

22
00:00:39,216 --> 00:00:41,525
the drive-in was where they saw their

23
00:00:41,525 --> 00:00:44,207
favorite movies, went on their first dates,

24
00:00:44,207 --> 00:00:46,204
and fell asleep in the back seat

25
00:00:46,204 --> 00:00:47,202
under the stars.

26
00:00:47,552 --> 00:00:50,201
Today, there are only about three hundred

27
00:00:50,201 --> 00:00:50,524
left.

28
00:00:50,704 --> 00:00:52,016
So what happened?

29
00:00:52,196 --> 00:00:54,744
Let's tell the story of the American

30
00:00:54,744 --> 00:00:56,514
drive-in, how it started,

31
00:00:56,514 --> 00:00:58,142
what it was like to go,

32
00:00:58,142 --> 00:01:00,903
and why almost all of them disappeared.

33
00:01:01,253 --> 00:01:03,678
The drive-in was invented by a man

34
00:01:03,678 --> 00:01:05,604
named Richard Hollingshead,

35
00:01:05,604 --> 00:01:07,174
in Camden, New Jersey.

36
00:01:07,354 --> 00:01:09,566
The story goes that his mother was

37
00:01:09,566 --> 00:01:11,973
a large woman who found movie theater

38
00:01:11,973 --> 00:01:13,275
seats uncomfortable.

39
00:01:13,455 --> 00:01:16,083
So he began experimenting in his own

40
00:01:16,083 --> 00:01:16,740
driveway.

41
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:19,089
He mounted a projector on the hood

42
00:01:19,089 --> 00:01:20,811
of his car and hung a sheet

43
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:21,959
between two trees.

44
00:01:22,309 --> 00:01:24,204
He tested how to park the cars

45
00:01:24,204 --> 00:01:25,594
so everyone could see.

46
00:01:25,774 --> 00:01:28,057
He put blocks under the front wheels

47
00:01:28,057 --> 00:01:29,071
to tilt them up.

48
00:01:29,251 --> 00:01:30,940
He tested a lawn sprinkler,

49
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:33,004
to see whether the movie could be

50
00:01:33,004 --> 00:01:34,255
watched in the rain.

51
00:01:34,435 --> 00:01:35,856
And on June sixth,

52
00:01:35,856 --> 00:01:39,883
nineteen thirty-three, he opened the first drive-in

53
00:01:39,883 --> 00:01:43,435
theater, on Crescent Boulevard in Pennsauken,

54
00:01:43,435 --> 00:01:44,304
New Jersey.

55
00:01:44,654 --> 00:01:47,728
Admission was twenty-five cents per car,

56
00:01:47,728 --> 00:01:50,342
plus twenty-five cents per person,

57
00:01:50,342 --> 00:01:52,572
with a maximum of one dollar.

58
00:01:52,752 --> 00:01:54,916
The first film was a British comedy

59
00:01:54,916 --> 00:01:56,153
called Wives Beware.

60
00:01:56,333 --> 00:01:57,993
His slogan was simple.

61
00:01:58,173 --> 00:02:00,157
The whole family is welcome,

62
00:02:00,157 --> 00:02:03,061
regardless of how noisy the children are.

63
00:02:03,412 --> 00:02:05,037
For the next ten years,

64
00:02:05,037 --> 00:02:06,593
drive-ins grew slowly.

65
00:02:06,773 --> 00:02:09,204
Then the Second World War ended,

66
00:02:09,204 --> 00:02:10,952
and everything changed.

67
00:02:11,132 --> 00:02:12,720
Soldiers came home,

68
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:14,975
got married and had babies.

69
00:02:15,155 --> 00:02:17,001
Families bought cars.

70
00:02:17,181 --> 00:02:19,689
New highways stretched into the countryside.

71
00:02:19,869 --> 00:02:22,025
And land at the edge of town

72
00:02:22,025 --> 00:02:22,795
was cheap.

73
00:02:23,145 --> 00:02:24,602
In nineteen forty-five,

74
00:02:24,602 --> 00:02:27,201
there were fewer than a hundred drive-ins

75
00:02:27,201 --> 00:02:28,532
in the whole country.

76
00:02:28,712 --> 00:02:30,242
By nineteen fifty-eight,

77
00:02:30,242 --> 00:02:32,473
there were more than four thousand.

78
00:02:32,653 --> 00:02:33,575
In some years,

79
00:02:33,575 --> 00:02:36,472
more people watched movies at drive-ins than

80
00:02:36,472 --> 00:02:38,249
at regular indoor theaters.

81
00:02:38,429 --> 00:02:41,188
It was one of the fastest-growing businesses

82
00:02:41,188 --> 00:02:41,877
in America.

83
00:02:42,228 --> 00:02:44,782
Why did families love it so much?

84
00:02:44,962 --> 00:02:45,867
It was cheap.

85
00:02:46,047 --> 00:02:48,442
Many drive-ins charged by the carload,

86
00:02:48,442 --> 00:02:50,206
so a family of six could get

87
00:02:50,206 --> 00:02:51,214
in for a dollar.

88
00:02:51,394 --> 00:02:53,008
You didn't need a babysitter.

89
00:02:53,188 --> 00:02:54,685
You didn't need to dress up.

90
00:02:54,865 --> 00:02:57,477
The kids could come in their pajamas,

91
00:02:57,477 --> 00:02:59,171
and if they fell asleep,

92
00:02:59,171 --> 00:03:00,159
that was fine.

93
00:03:00,510 --> 00:03:02,088
Mothers loved it too.

94
00:03:02,268 --> 00:03:04,699
A night at the indoor movies meant

95
00:03:04,699 --> 00:03:06,486
dressing the children up,

96
00:03:06,486 --> 00:03:09,560
keeping them quiet and hoping nobody cried.

97
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:10,985
At the drive-in,

98
00:03:10,985 --> 00:03:12,308
a baby could cry,

99
00:03:12,308 --> 00:03:15,187
a toddler could climb over the seats,

100
00:03:15,187 --> 00:03:17,600
and nobody else had to hear it.

101
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:20,953
Some drive-ins even warmed bottles at the

102
00:03:20,953 --> 00:03:23,352
snack bar, and a few had diaper

103
00:03:23,352 --> 00:03:24,049
services.

104
00:03:24,399 --> 00:03:26,314
Going to the drive-in had a ritual.

105
00:03:26,494 --> 00:03:27,886
You arrived early,

106
00:03:27,886 --> 00:03:30,360
before dark, to get a good spot.

107
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:31,614
Not too close,

108
00:03:31,614 --> 00:03:32,535
not too far,

109
00:03:32,535 --> 00:03:34,070
right in the middle.

110
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:36,167
Dad pulled the car up onto the

111
00:03:36,167 --> 00:03:38,532
little hill, so the front wheels were

112
00:03:38,532 --> 00:03:40,322
tilted up toward the screen.

113
00:03:40,502 --> 00:03:42,104
Then you rolled down the window.

114
00:03:42,454 --> 00:03:44,642
Next to every parking space was a

115
00:03:44,642 --> 00:03:46,366
metal post with a speaker.

116
00:03:46,546 --> 00:03:48,621
You took the speaker off the post,

117
00:03:48,621 --> 00:03:50,512
hooked it over your car window,

118
00:03:50,512 --> 00:03:52,038
and turned up the volume.

119
00:03:52,218 --> 00:03:53,707
The sound was tinny,

120
00:03:53,707 --> 00:03:54,899
and it crackled,

121
00:03:54,899 --> 00:03:56,165
but nobody cared.

122
00:03:56,345 --> 00:03:57,364
Every so often,

123
00:03:57,364 --> 00:03:59,877
someone forgot it was there and drove

124
00:03:59,877 --> 00:04:02,390
away with the speaker still attached,

125
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:04,632
pulling the wire right out of the

126
00:04:04,632 --> 00:04:04,971
post.

127
00:04:05,321 --> 00:04:06,413
Before the movie,

128
00:04:06,413 --> 00:04:08,853
kids ran to the playground right under

129
00:04:08,853 --> 00:04:09,559
the screen.

130
00:04:09,739 --> 00:04:11,873
Many drive-ins had swings,

131
00:04:11,873 --> 00:04:13,926
slides, a merry-go-round,

132
00:04:13,926 --> 00:04:16,717
even little trains and pony rides.

133
00:04:16,897 --> 00:04:18,684
Parents sat on the hood of the

134
00:04:18,684 --> 00:04:21,068
car and talked with the neighbors parked

135
00:04:21,068 --> 00:04:21,843
next to them.

136
00:04:22,023 --> 00:04:23,567
Then the sky got dark,

137
00:04:23,567 --> 00:04:25,884
the lights came up on the screen,

138
00:04:25,884 --> 00:04:28,060
and kids raced back to the car.

139
00:04:28,409 --> 00:04:30,232
And then there was the snack bar.

140
00:04:30,412 --> 00:04:32,001
In the drive-in business,

141
00:04:32,001 --> 00:04:34,036
it was said that the owners made

142
00:04:34,036 --> 00:04:36,133
more money on the popcorn than on

143
00:04:36,133 --> 00:04:36,833
the movies.

144
00:04:37,013 --> 00:04:39,589
Some drive-ins earned more than half of

145
00:04:39,589 --> 00:04:41,506
their money at the snack bar.

146
00:04:41,686 --> 00:04:43,199
So they did everything they could to

147
00:04:43,199 --> 00:04:43,787
get you there.

148
00:04:44,136 --> 00:04:47,346
Between the movies came the famous intermission

149
00:04:47,346 --> 00:04:47,619
ads.

150
00:04:47,799 --> 00:04:50,710
A dancing hot dog jumping into a

151
00:04:50,710 --> 00:04:51,073
bun.

152
00:04:51,253 --> 00:04:53,692
A singing box of popcorn.

153
00:04:53,872 --> 00:04:56,168
A clock counting down the minutes until

154
00:04:56,168 --> 00:04:57,169
the next feature.

155
00:04:57,349 --> 00:04:58,951
Let's all go to the lobby,

156
00:04:58,951 --> 00:05:01,168
they sang, to get ourselves a treat.

157
00:05:01,348 --> 00:05:04,142
Those little cartoons are still burned into

158
00:05:04,142 --> 00:05:06,480
the memory of millions of Americans.

159
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:09,699
The snack bar sold hot dogs,

160
00:05:09,699 --> 00:05:12,466
hamburgers, popcorn, candy,

161
00:05:12,466 --> 00:05:14,515
pizza, French fries,

162
00:05:14,515 --> 00:05:17,998
ice cream and soft drinks in waxed

163
00:05:17,998 --> 00:05:19,125
paper cups.

164
00:05:19,305 --> 00:05:22,208
Some families brought their own food,

165
00:05:22,208 --> 00:05:24,561
packed in a cooler or a picnic

166
00:05:24,561 --> 00:05:25,110
basket.

167
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:28,395
Fried chicken, sandwiches and a thermos of

168
00:05:28,395 --> 00:05:28,913
coffee.

169
00:05:29,093 --> 00:05:30,656
The owners didn't love it,

170
00:05:30,656 --> 00:05:32,100
but they rarely said no.

171
00:05:32,449 --> 00:05:35,109
Most drive-ins showed a double feature,

172
00:05:35,109 --> 00:05:37,291
two movies for the price of one.

173
00:05:37,471 --> 00:05:39,452
The first one started at dusk,

174
00:05:39,452 --> 00:05:41,764
and the second one ended long after

175
00:05:41,764 --> 00:05:42,359
midnight.

176
00:05:42,539 --> 00:05:43,705
In the fifties,

177
00:05:43,705 --> 00:05:46,270
that might be a western with John

178
00:05:46,270 --> 00:05:49,069
Wayne, a monster movie like The Blob

179
00:05:49,069 --> 00:05:50,546
or Them, a musical,

180
00:05:50,546 --> 00:05:53,034
or a Disney film for the family.

181
00:05:53,383 --> 00:05:56,528
Drive-ins became famous for monster movies and

182
00:05:56,528 --> 00:05:57,621
science fiction.

183
00:05:57,801 --> 00:05:59,941
Giant ants, giant spiders,

184
00:05:59,941 --> 00:06:03,314
invaders from Mars and creatures from the

185
00:06:03,314 --> 00:06:04,384
black lagoon.

186
00:06:04,564 --> 00:06:08,759
Low-budget studios made films especially for drive-ins

187
00:06:08,759 --> 00:06:09,847
and teenagers.

188
00:06:10,027 --> 00:06:12,157
Kids would be scared out of their

189
00:06:12,157 --> 00:06:14,287
wits, then sleep in the back seat

190
00:06:14,287 --> 00:06:15,448
on the drive home.

191
00:06:15,799 --> 00:06:17,013
On some nights,

192
00:06:17,013 --> 00:06:19,847
the drive-in stayed open all night,

193
00:06:19,847 --> 00:06:23,005
showing three, four or even five movies

194
00:06:23,005 --> 00:06:23,734
in a row,

195
00:06:23,734 --> 00:06:25,191
from dusk to dawn.

196
00:06:25,371 --> 00:06:26,168
At the end,

197
00:06:26,168 --> 00:06:28,053
the sky was turning light,

198
00:06:28,053 --> 00:06:30,807
and the owners might serve free coffee

199
00:06:30,807 --> 00:06:33,417
and doughnuts to anyone still awake.

200
00:06:33,766 --> 00:06:35,195
And there were the mosquitoes.

201
00:06:35,375 --> 00:06:37,300
On a hot night with the windows

202
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:39,287
down, they came by the hundreds.

203
00:06:39,467 --> 00:06:43,103
Drive-ins sold mosquito coils that you burned

204
00:06:43,103 --> 00:06:44,476
on the dashboard,

205
00:06:44,476 --> 00:06:47,304
filling the car with a smoky smell.

206
00:06:47,484 --> 00:06:50,458
Some even sprayed the lot with insecticide

207
00:06:50,458 --> 00:06:52,511
from a truck before the show.

208
00:06:52,691 --> 00:06:55,826
Almost everyone who went remembers the bites.

209
00:06:56,175 --> 00:06:59,245
For teenagers, the drive-in meant something else

210
00:06:59,245 --> 00:06:59,821
entirely.

211
00:07:00,001 --> 00:07:01,814
It was the most private place a

212
00:07:01,814 --> 00:07:03,217
young couple could find.

213
00:07:03,397 --> 00:07:04,929
A car of their own,

214
00:07:04,929 --> 00:07:05,896
in the dark,

215
00:07:05,896 --> 00:07:07,751
far from their parents.

216
00:07:07,931 --> 00:07:11,322
Not surprisingly, adults called the drive-in the

217
00:07:11,322 --> 00:07:14,219
passion pit, and some towns worried about

218
00:07:14,219 --> 00:07:15,349
it a great deal.

219
00:07:15,699 --> 00:07:17,580
Owners tried to keep order.

220
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:20,570
Ushers walked the rows with flashlights.

221
00:07:20,750 --> 00:07:24,349
Some drive-ins had rules against fogged-up windows.

222
00:07:24,529 --> 00:07:27,191
And teenagers found ways around the price

223
00:07:27,191 --> 00:07:28,035
of admission.

224
00:07:28,215 --> 00:07:30,230
The classic trick was to hide two

225
00:07:30,230 --> 00:07:32,061
or three friends in the trunk,

226
00:07:32,061 --> 00:07:34,136
then let them out once you parked.

227
00:07:34,316 --> 00:07:36,884
Many grown-ups today will admit they did

228
00:07:36,884 --> 00:07:37,718
exactly that.

229
00:07:38,067 --> 00:07:39,080
For many couples,

230
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:41,225
the drive-in was where it all began.

231
00:07:41,405 --> 00:07:43,751
A first date in a borrowed car.

232
00:07:43,931 --> 00:07:46,322
A first kiss during the second feature.

233
00:07:46,502 --> 00:07:48,697
A marriage, years later.

234
00:07:48,877 --> 00:07:50,929
Ask anyone who was a teenager in

235
00:07:50,929 --> 00:07:52,405
the fifties or sixties,

236
00:07:52,405 --> 00:07:54,971
and they probably have a drive-in story,

237
00:07:54,971 --> 00:07:57,153
though they might not tell you all

238
00:07:57,153 --> 00:07:57,538
of it.

239
00:07:57,887 --> 00:08:00,662
Drive-ins came in every size.

240
00:08:00,842 --> 00:08:01,909
In small towns,

241
00:08:01,909 --> 00:08:04,256
there might be a single screen in

242
00:08:04,256 --> 00:08:04,824
a field.

243
00:08:05,004 --> 00:08:06,226
Near the big cities,

244
00:08:06,226 --> 00:08:08,670
there were giant drive-ins with room for

245
00:08:08,670 --> 00:08:10,380
more than two thousand cars.

246
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:13,093
The All-Weather Drive-In in Copiague,

247
00:08:13,093 --> 00:08:16,106
New York, covered twenty-eight acres and had

248
00:08:16,106 --> 00:08:17,680
an indoor viewing area,

249
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:19,871
a cafeteria and a shuttle train.

250
00:08:20,221 --> 00:08:23,015
Some drive-ins had a section for walk-in

251
00:08:23,015 --> 00:08:24,552
viewers, with benches.

252
00:08:24,732 --> 00:08:27,817
Some had fly-in sections for small private

253
00:08:27,817 --> 00:08:28,552
airplanes.

254
00:08:28,732 --> 00:08:31,884
In winter, a few northern drive-ins offered

255
00:08:31,884 --> 00:08:34,744
electric car heaters that plugged in at

256
00:08:34,744 --> 00:08:35,477
each spot.

257
00:08:35,657 --> 00:08:38,268
And there were drive-ins with laundromats,

258
00:08:38,268 --> 00:08:40,194
so you could do the wash during

259
00:08:40,194 --> 00:08:40,754
the show.

260
00:08:41,103 --> 00:08:42,357
So what happened?

261
00:08:42,537 --> 00:08:44,627
The first blow was television.

262
00:08:44,807 --> 00:08:46,216
By the late fifties,

263
00:08:46,216 --> 00:08:48,118
almost every home had a TV,

264
00:08:48,118 --> 00:08:50,866
and families could watch shows for free

265
00:08:50,866 --> 00:08:53,120
without leaving the living room.

266
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:55,842
Attendance at all movie theaters dropped.

267
00:08:56,022 --> 00:08:58,585
Drive-ins, which depended on families,

268
00:08:58,585 --> 00:08:59,529
were hit hard.

269
00:08:59,878 --> 00:09:02,630
The second blow was daylight saving time.

270
00:09:02,810 --> 00:09:04,983
As more states adopted it in the

271
00:09:04,983 --> 00:09:07,360
sixties, the summer sun set an hour

272
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:07,767
later.

273
00:09:07,947 --> 00:09:11,190
Since a drive-in couldn't start until dark,

274
00:09:11,190 --> 00:09:14,056
the first movie began later and later,

275
00:09:14,056 --> 00:09:16,167
sometimes after nine-thirty.

276
00:09:16,347 --> 00:09:19,111
Families with young children stopped going.

277
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:21,179
The third blow was land.

278
00:09:21,359 --> 00:09:23,968
Drive-ins had been built on cheap land

279
00:09:23,968 --> 00:09:25,341
at the edge of town.

280
00:09:25,521 --> 00:09:27,284
But as the suburbs grew,

281
00:09:27,284 --> 00:09:29,561
that land became very valuable.

282
00:09:29,741 --> 00:09:32,255
A drive-in that made money only in

283
00:09:32,255 --> 00:09:35,212
summer could not compete with a shopping

284
00:09:35,212 --> 00:09:38,391
center, a housing development or a highway.

285
00:09:38,571 --> 00:09:40,461
Owners were offered more for the land

286
00:09:40,461 --> 00:09:42,147
than the theater could ever earn.

287
00:09:42,496 --> 00:09:43,772
In the seventies,

288
00:09:43,772 --> 00:09:47,372
many struggling drive-ins turned to cheap action

289
00:09:47,372 --> 00:09:49,097
movies and adult films,

290
00:09:49,097 --> 00:09:51,947
which made them unwelcome to families.

291
00:09:52,127 --> 00:09:54,722
Then came cable television and the video

292
00:09:54,722 --> 00:09:57,511
cassette recorder in the late seventies and

293
00:09:57,511 --> 00:09:58,095
eighties.

294
00:09:58,275 --> 00:10:00,302
Now people could watch movies at home

295
00:10:00,302 --> 00:10:01,398
whenever they liked.

296
00:10:01,578 --> 00:10:02,840
By nineteen ninety,

297
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:05,629
fewer than a thousand drive-ins were left.

298
00:10:05,979 --> 00:10:08,000
The last blow came in the two

299
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,692
thousand tens, when Hollywood stopped shipping movies

300
00:10:11,692 --> 00:10:14,687
on film and switched to digital projection.

301
00:10:14,867 --> 00:10:18,103
Converting a drive-in to digital cost tens

302
00:10:18,103 --> 00:10:19,952
of thousands of dollars.

303
00:10:20,132 --> 00:10:21,233
For many small,

304
00:10:21,233 --> 00:10:24,903
family-owned drive-ins that only opened in summer,

305
00:10:24,903 --> 00:10:26,738
that was simply too much.

306
00:10:27,088 --> 00:10:29,538
But the drive-in never completely died.

307
00:10:29,718 --> 00:10:32,905
Today, about three hundred are still operating

308
00:10:32,905 --> 00:10:33,944
across America.

309
00:10:34,124 --> 00:10:36,270
Many are run by families who have

310
00:10:36,270 --> 00:10:38,025
owned them for generations.

311
00:10:38,205 --> 00:10:41,775
Pennsylvania, Ohio, New York and Indiana have

312
00:10:41,775 --> 00:10:42,489
the most.

313
00:10:42,669 --> 00:10:44,207
And on a summer weekend,

314
00:10:44,207 --> 00:10:46,001
many of them are still full.

315
00:10:46,351 --> 00:10:49,074
Shankweiler's Drive-In in Orefield,

316
00:10:49,074 --> 00:10:52,574
Pennsylvania, opened in nineteen thirty-four.

317
00:10:52,754 --> 00:10:55,808
It's the oldest drive-in still operating in

318
00:10:55,808 --> 00:10:56,377
America.

319
00:10:56,557 --> 00:10:58,905
Bengies Drive-In, near Baltimore,

320
00:10:58,905 --> 00:11:01,252
has one of the biggest screens in

321
00:11:01,252 --> 00:11:02,106
the country.

322
00:11:02,286 --> 00:11:03,742
And in twenty twenty,

323
00:11:03,742 --> 00:11:05,128
during the pandemic,

324
00:11:05,128 --> 00:11:07,623
drive-ins had a surprising comeback,

325
00:11:07,623 --> 00:11:09,910
as people looked for safe ways to

326
00:11:09,910 --> 00:11:11,365
see a movie together.

327
00:11:11,715 --> 00:11:14,542
Today, instead of window speakers,

328
00:11:14,542 --> 00:11:16,870
you tune your car radio to a

329
00:11:16,870 --> 00:11:18,449
special FM station.

330
00:11:18,629 --> 00:11:20,278
The movies are digital.

331
00:11:20,458 --> 00:11:22,966
But the rest is surprisingly the same.

332
00:11:23,146 --> 00:11:25,061
Families in pajamas.

333
00:11:25,241 --> 00:11:28,414
A snack bar with popcorn and hot

334
00:11:28,414 --> 00:11:28,910
dogs.

335
00:11:29,090 --> 00:11:31,981
A dancing hot dog between the features.

336
00:11:32,161 --> 00:11:34,497
And kids falling asleep in the back

337
00:11:34,497 --> 00:11:35,899
seat under the stars.

338
00:11:36,249 --> 00:11:38,093
For those who grew up with it,

339
00:11:38,093 --> 00:11:40,243
the drive-in was more than a movie.

340
00:11:40,423 --> 00:11:41,979
It was summer itself.

341
00:11:42,159 --> 00:11:45,189
The smell of popcorn and bug spray.

342
00:11:45,369 --> 00:11:47,757
The sound of crickets and the crackle

343
00:11:47,757 --> 00:11:48,724
of the speaker.

344
00:11:48,904 --> 00:11:50,750
The stars above the screen.

345
00:11:50,930 --> 00:11:53,203
A family together in the car,

346
00:11:53,203 --> 00:11:55,319
laughing at the same jokes.

347
00:11:55,669 --> 00:11:57,941
And that's why the drive-in is still

348
00:11:57,941 --> 00:12:00,022
one of the most loved memories of

349
00:12:00,022 --> 00:12:00,906
American life.

350
00:12:01,086 --> 00:12:03,450
It belonged to a time when we

351
00:12:03,450 --> 00:12:04,510
had big cars,

352
00:12:04,510 --> 00:12:07,446
cheap gas, open land and long summer

353
00:12:07,446 --> 00:12:08,017
nights.

354
00:12:08,197 --> 00:12:10,032
It was a time when going out

355
00:12:10,032 --> 00:12:12,457
to the movies meant the whole family,

356
00:12:12,457 --> 00:12:14,292
piled into the car together.

357
00:12:14,641 --> 00:12:16,627
So tell us in the comments.

358
00:12:16,807 --> 00:12:18,537
What was the name of your drive-in?

359
00:12:18,717 --> 00:12:21,001
What movie do you remember seeing there,

360
00:12:21,001 --> 00:12:22,258
and who were you with?

361
00:12:22,438 --> 00:12:24,157
Did you ever sneak someone in the

362
00:12:24,157 --> 00:12:24,469
trunk?

363
00:12:24,649 --> 00:12:26,019
We read every comment.

364
00:12:26,199 --> 00:12:28,148
Next time, we're looking at why the

365
00:12:28,148 --> 00:12:30,263
fifties were harder than you remember.

366
00:12:30,443 --> 00:12:32,236
Thank you for spending this time with

367
00:12:32,236 --> 00:12:32,382
me.
